{"parentUuid":"9404ab3d-fd93-4f22-adb7-a1465fdd6a04","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d7fd903f-4eab-4718-8a3d-1c392317615c","agentId":"abfed2a994c69f1f0","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019ruw71xvF1dHqSWrYBYHCM","type":"tool_result","content":"# FunSearch Bin Packing Example\n\nBased on my review of the PDF content, I cannot locate the specific technical details you requested about the bin packing implementation. While the document mentions bin packing as one of FunSearch's application areas, it does not provide:\n\n1. The exact Python function signature for the priority function evolved by the LLM\n2. The evaluator code used to score candidate solutions\n3. Specific input parameters (item size, bin capacity, etc.)\n4. The output format of the evolved function\n5. Number of LLM samples or wall-clock timing data\n\nThe document appears to be a high-level review or analysis of FunSearch's contributions to mathematical problems, discussing bin packing conceptually rather than providing implementation details. To obtain these technical specifics, you would need to consult the original FunSearch research paper or technical documentation that contains the actual experimental setup and code.\n\n[Binary content (application/pdf, 188.3KB) also saved to C:\\Users\\22957\\.claude\\projects\\D--claude-pj-zmd\\7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9\\tool-results\\webfetch-1778185831314-2iw8d5.pdf]"}]},"uuid":"ecee2267-0d6c-4607-be76-c76d79d1b457","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:30:34.755Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"9404ab3d-fd93-4f22-adb7-a1465fdd6a04","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"ecee2267-0d6c-4607-be76-c76d79d1b457","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"abfed2a994c69f1f0","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01M75pwmShen6BU3E8jgc4W2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_016cQEg5wBV1aV35EBysJqBW","name":"WebFetch","input":{"prompt":"Extract from the ShinkaEvolve paper/blog: (1) sample efficiency numbers (how many LLM calls for circle packing 26 — they claim 150 samples), (2) comparison vs OpenEvolve and AlphaEvolve in samples needed, (3) what the framework does differently (UCB bandits, novelty rejection, etc.), (4) cost in dollars for benchmark runs","url":"https://sakana.ai/shinka-evolve/"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":31302,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":873,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":31302},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":873,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation_input_tokens":31302,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":31302,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}},"requestId":"req_011CaoqodV2qSU4cnBAooSUt","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"50b079ab-27a3-4d33-bf1c-4644cdb5dc11","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:30:35.707Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"626bbb8b-359a-460a-ae6c-e39959ad24f3","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d7fd903f-4eab-4718-8a3d-1c392317615c","agentId":"abfed2a994c69f1f0","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01U37N9dEZ5PdigNuJfzNBtm","type":"tool_result","content":"# OpenEvolve Circle Packing Example Analysis\n\nBased on the provided GitHub file, here's what I can extract:\n\n## Program Structure\n\n**Initial Program:**\nThe file shows a `construct_packing()` function that arranges 26 circles in a unit square using:\n- Linear radius distribution: `np.linspace(0.12, 0.05, n)`\n- Hexagonal grid initialization with random placement\n- SLSQP optimization with overlap and boundary constraints\n- Radius bounds of `(0.03, 0.2)` per circle\n\n## What's Missing\n\nThe content provided only displays the **final evolved program**, not:\n- The initial baseline program structure\n- The evaluator function definition\n- Configuration parameters\n- Number of iterations performed\n- LLM cost information\n- Side-by-side comparison of initial vs. evolved versions\n\n## Key Observable Outcome\n\nThe file includes a comment stating: *\"AlphaEvolve improved this to 2.635\"* - indicating the sum of all radii achieved approximately 2.635 after evolution.\n\n## Limitation\n\nTo provide the complete analysis you requested (evaluator structure, iterations, costs, initial vs. evolved comparison), the GitHub page would need to display additional documentation or configuration files not shown in the current excerpt."}]},"uuid":"16c9d298-2ad9-415b-887d-9f2e1e33e625","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:30:36.400Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"626bbb8b-359a-460a-ae6c-e39959ad24f3","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"6d98a8f7-925f-4b8c-96c0-1e34708e2d6e","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d7fd903f-4eab-4718-8a3d-1c392317615c","agentId":"abfed2a994c69f1f0","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Vkq1wk4m2SggDNQwJqFAHw","type":"tool_result","content":"# AlphaEvolve: Technical Details from Abstract\n\nBased on the provided abstract, I cannot find explicit information addressing your specific questions:\n\n**FunSearch Comparison**: The abstract does not detail technical contributions over FunSearch (Romera-Paredes et al., 2023), only noting that AlphaEvolve \"significantly expands the scope of prior automated discovery methods.\"\n\n**Evaluator Interface**: The abstract mentions AlphaEvolve receives \"continuously receiving feedback from one or more evaluators\" but provides no specification of how this interface functions.\n\n**Code Evolution Scope**: The abstract does not specify whether the system evolves entire files or code snippets during its iterative process.\n\n**Iteration/Cost Numbers**: No metrics regarding iterations or computational costs are included in the abstract for any benchmarks.\n\n**Key Results Mentioned**:\n- Data center scheduling algorithm optimization\n- Hardware accelerator circuit simplification\n- Matrix multiplication: \"a search algorithm that found a procedure to multiply two 4×4 complex-valued matrices using 48 scalar multiplications; offering the first improvement, after 56 years, over Strassen's algorithm\"\n\nFor detailed technical specifications on these topics, the full paper PDF would be necessary."}]},"uuid":"5988ec43-d936-4bac-a8f7-9ccac87ea63d","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:30:38.389Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"6d98a8f7-925f-4b8c-96c0-1e34708e2d6e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"50b079ab-27a3-4d33-bf1c-4644cdb5dc11","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d7fd903f-4eab-4718-8a3d-1c392317615c","agentId":"abfed2a994c69f1f0","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016cQEg5wBV1aV35EBysJqBW","type":"tool_result","content":"# ShinkaEvolve Key Technical Details\n\n## Sample Efficiency Numbers\nThe framework achieved **150 samples** for the 26-circle packing problem, representing a dramatic improvement over prior approaches. For the AIME math reasoning task, it required only **75 generations**.\n\n## Comparisons to Prior Work\nThe blog states that ShinkaEvolve provides \"a sample-efficient alternative to AlphaEvolve and outperforms its Circle Packing solution.\" However, specific numerical comparisons between sample counts for AlphaEvolve are not provided in the available content.\n\n## Key Technical Innovations\nShinkaEvolve differentiates itself through three mechanisms:\n\n1. **Parent Sampling Strategy**: \"A program parent sampling technique that intelligently balances exploiting known good solutions and exploring new ideas\"\n\n2. **Novelty-Based Rejection**: The framework employs \"code novelty rejection-sampling to avoid wasting time evaluating minor, uninteresting variations\" using embedding similarities and LLM-based creativity assessment\n\n3. **Adaptive LLM Selection**: \"A bandit-based strategy that dynamically selects the best LLM from an ensemble for the task at hand\"\n\n## Cost Data\nThe document does not include dollar figures or computational cost estimates for running these benchmarks. Cost metrics are not disclosed in the provided material."}]},"uuid":"9ea374f4-1423-417c-9620-994e4b6b2634","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:30:41.253Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"50b079ab-27a3-4d33-bf1c-4644cdb5dc11","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
